WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.120
Seeing dead people, becoming a neuroscientist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:08.880
and foiling burglars can be exhausting -&nbsp;
turns out some child stars just need a break!

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:15.040
Anna Chlumsky won America's hearts when she&nbsp;
starred as Vada Sultenfuss in the 1991 film "My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:21.440
Girl," only her second project after a tiny part&nbsp;
in 1989's "Uncle Buck." Chlumsky followed that up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:27.520
with a less successful but still charming sequel&nbsp;
"My Girl 2" and similar roles in "Trading Mom"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:33.520
and "Gold Diggers: The Secret of Bear Mountain."&nbsp;
Things started to dry up for Chlumsky around 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.280
and she stopped acting entirely&nbsp;
in 1998 when she went to college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.080
telling People magazine in February 2022 -

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.000
"As a kid, you're just along for the&nbsp;
ride, but it can be extremely damaging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.960
[In college] I had this out-of-body moment where&nbsp;
I realized, 'I don't have to do this anymore.'"

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.680
"I don't have to FEEL like this anymore. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.800
I don't have to feel like I won't book&nbsp;
something, like I'm not good enough."

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.960
After graduating from the University of&nbsp;
Chicago, Chlumsky told People that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.160
did not immediately return to acting, finding&nbsp;
entry-level journalism jobs, but eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.560
enrolled in acting school. She returned&nbsp;
to the business with a short film in 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.400
but hit it big in 2012 when she scored a main&nbsp;
role in HBO's political comedy "Veep." Chlumsky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:20.080
received six Primetime Emmy Award nominations&nbsp;
for playing Amy Brookheimer in the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.400
and it remains the highlight of her career.&nbsp;
She has since starred in the Netflix miniseries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.000
"Inventing Anna," and can most recently be&nbsp;
seen in Peacock's original film, "They/Them."

00:01:30.139 --> 00:01:36.320
As far as child stars go, Mayim Bialik has always&nbsp;
seemed to have her head screwed on straight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.520
She began in show business before she even hit&nbsp;
her teens, appearing in TV series like "Webster"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.880
and "Molloy" before becoming super famous as the&nbsp;
titular character in the sitcom "Blossom," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:53.840
aired from 1990 to 1995. Despite her success as a&nbsp;
kid actor, Bialik expressed mixed feelings about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:02:00.000
children acting professionally. In a post for her&nbsp;
website Grok Nation, Bialik discussed how she sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.960
theater as valuable for kids, but how&nbsp;
performing on cue can take an emotional toll.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:11.520
Bialik largely stopped acting for a decade, from&nbsp;
1995 to 2005, except for some voice work and two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.640
or three small guest spots in low-profile shows&nbsp;
like "Welcome to Paradox." She had a memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:22.800
three-episode stint on "Curb Your Enthusiasm" in&nbsp;
2005 and played a doctor in 10 episodes of "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.320
Secret Life of the American Teenager," but it was&nbsp;
Bialik's casting as Amy Farrah Fowler in "The Big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.920
Bang Theory" that completely altered her career.&nbsp;
She joined the show in 2010, appeared in more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.840
200 episodes, and received four Primetime Emmy&nbsp;
Award nominations in addition to other awards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.880
She currently has her own sitcom, "Call Me&nbsp;
Kat," and hosts "Jeopardy" in her spare time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:50.320
Perhaps most impressively, Bialik used her time&nbsp;
away from Hollywood to earn multiple degrees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.534
including a doctorate in neuroscience&nbsp;
that she completed in 2007 at UCLA.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.520
When it comes to child actors, Macauley Culkin is&nbsp;
arguably one of the most famous there ever was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.320
Culkin was just five years old when he made his&nbsp;
first uncredited appearance in a film but became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:11.840
massively famous at age 10 with his iconic&nbsp;
role as Kevin McCallister in the 1990 film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.520
"Home Alone." Over the next handful of years, he&nbsp;
racked up many credits, starring in films such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.560
"My Girl," "The Good Son," and "The Pagemaster."&nbsp;
After appearing as the title character in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.920
"Richie Rich" film in 1994, Culkin stepped&nbsp;
away from acting for nearly a full decade.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.120
Culkin "retired" from acting in his early teens&nbsp;
because he no longer wanted to do it, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.400
because it allowed him to stop bankrolling&nbsp;
his parents. He became emancipated at age 14&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:44.800
and has said his father was abusive. As he told&nbsp;
comedian Marc Maron on his "WTF" podcast in 2018,

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.960
"I had been wanting to take a break for a while&nbsp;
and eventually I was like, 'I'm done guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.480
hope you all made your money because&nbsp;
there is no more coming from me.'"

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.600
"You guys give up, or ya thirsty for more?"

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.560
Culkin returned to the screen on his&nbsp;
own terms in 2003, with an acclaimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.640
role in the film "Party Monster." He has&nbsp;
since had memorable parts in movies like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.801
"Saved!" and in television series&nbsp;
such as "American Horror Story."

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.800
Given how successful Macauley Culkin was as a&nbsp;
child, many people forget that his siblings Rory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.560
and Kieran both acted as children as well. Kieran,&nbsp;
in particular, has been able to achieve tremendous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.680
success as an adult after a time away from the&nbsp;
limelight. Kieran, the eldest by two years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:30.320
began his acting career with a small part in "Home&nbsp;
Alone," after which he worked steadily in films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.960
like "Father of the Bride," "Only the Lonely,"&nbsp;
and "Nowhere to Run." He notched more than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.720
dozen screen credits by the time he turned 18 in&nbsp;
2000 and, two years later, established himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.760
with an acclaimed role in "Igby Goes Down," for&nbsp;
which he was nominated a Golden Globe Award.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.360
After "Igby," Kieran did not appear&nbsp;
on screen for six years — though he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.200
did have a few stage roles during that&nbsp;
time, telling The Hollywood Reporter -

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.760
"I would not have been able to&nbsp;
handle whatever kind of success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:59.360
or attention came from that. So I&nbsp;
quite literally ran away from it."

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:04.720
Culkin returned to acting full-time in 2008,&nbsp;
and his career hit new heights in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:09.920
when he originated the role of Roman Roy on&nbsp;
the successful HBO show "Succession." For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:14.720
his work on the series thus far, Culkin has&nbsp;
been nominated for one Primetime Emmy Award,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.834
three Golden Globe Awards, and one&nbsp;
individual Screen Actors Guild Award.

00:05:18.834 --> 00:05:24.400
She is nearly 60 now, so younger generations&nbsp;
probably do not know how big of a star Brooke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:29.120
Shields was back when she was young. According&nbsp;
to Women's Wear Daily, Shields was less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:33.680
a year old when she got her start in modeling&nbsp;
and she continued to model throughout her early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:39.040
life. Shields was famously part of a provocative&nbsp;
campaign for Calvin Klein Jeans as a teenager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.240
is the youngest person ever to land on the cover&nbsp;
of Vogue, and her eyebrows alone have inspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:49.360
many a beauty article. But Shields is not just&nbsp;
known as a model, and her acting career began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:55.920
similarly early. She controversially played a&nbsp;
child sex worker in the 1978 film "Pretty Baby,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.960
and as a teen, appeared in fare like "The&nbsp;
Blue Lagoon," "Endless Love," and "Sahara."

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.640
After professionally working&nbsp;
for literally her entire life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:10.320
Shields took some time off between 1984&nbsp;
and 1988 to attend Princeton University,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:15.040
from which she graduated with honors, according&nbsp;
to The Chicago Tribune. She re-entered Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:21.120
upon her graduation and, in 1996, landed her&nbsp;
biggest role to date, the lead in the sitcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.520
"Suddenly Susan," which aired for four&nbsp;
seasons and earned Shields two Golden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:30.880
Globe Award nominations. Shields has continued to&nbsp;
work steadily in the years since her sitcom ended,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:35.935
appearing mostly in television in shows like&nbsp;
"Lipstick Jungle," and "Jane the Virgin."

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:41.600
The list of children who have been nominated&nbsp;
for Oscars is short, so Haley Joel Osment's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:48.240
2000 Academy Award nod for his work in "The Sixth&nbsp;
Sense" is significant. Osment was 11 years old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.800
at the time of his nomination, and he remains&nbsp;
one of the youngest nominees of all time. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.760
actor had already been in show business for half a&nbsp;
decade, starting with commercials and very quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:02.720
transitioning to film. One of his first parts&nbsp;
was playing Forrest's son in "Forrest Gump," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:07.600
he also had main roles on the television series&nbsp;
"Thunder Alley" and "The Jeff Foxworthy Show."

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.640
Osment had nearly two dozen acting credits&nbsp;
before "The Sixth Sense," but that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:18.000
role that took him to the next level. After his&nbsp;
Oscar nom, Osment had high-profile leading roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.960
in films such as "Pay It Forward" and "A.I.&nbsp;
Artificial Intelligence." He never officially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:29.840
retired from acting, but Osment took on mostly&nbsp;
voice work between the years of 2005 and 2012.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:33.440
"It's cold! Donald, gimme a coat!"

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:40.320
Though he got into a bit of trouble with a DUI&nbsp;
at age 18, Osment got things back on track and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:45.120
graduated from New York University during his&nbsp;
acting hiatus. He started appearing on-camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:50.720
again in 2013, starting with the television series&nbsp;
"Alpha House," and has established himself well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:56.301
an adult thespian with roles in television series&nbsp;
such as "Future Man" and "The Kominsky Method."

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:01.680
Andrea Barber started her career in 1982,&nbsp;
at age six, when she began appearing as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:07.600
Carrie Brady on the soap opera "Days of Our&nbsp;
Lives." She remained on "Days" until 1986,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.960
during which time she also did guest stints on&nbsp;
other television series. Her true claim to fame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.920
however, is her scene-stealing role as D.J.&nbsp;
Tanner's best friend and neighbor Kimmy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:25.040
Gibbler on the iconic sitcom "Full House." Barber&nbsp;
appeared in 146 episodes of the show between 1987&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:30.400
and 1995 — and then left the entertainment&nbsp;
business. As she told E! News in 2020 -

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:33.920
"I know a lot of people who play&nbsp;
outlandish characters like this do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.240
struggle with typecasting or just being&nbsp;
seen as that. I never struggled. I left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.600
the business at 18 just because&nbsp;
my heart wasn't in acting anymore"

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.080
Thankfully for us "Full House" fans, Barber&nbsp;
was willing to return to the role that made her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:51.920
famous when Netflix launched a spinoff, "Fuller&nbsp;
House," in 2016. The show ran for five seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:55.040
but Barber may stick with acting&nbsp;
this time, telling E! News -

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.160
"I've got the bug now. I would&nbsp;
love to continue if it's still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.080
doing comedy because I think that's&nbsp;
my sweet spot and I love it so much."

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:07.741
Barber has since appeared in guest roles on&nbsp;
two series, "Minutiae" and "That Girl Lay Lay."

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:13.920
Twins have always been really popular amongst&nbsp;
child actors, particularly babies and toddlers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:19.200
as labor laws limit the amount of time very young&nbsp;
children can spend on a set. Along with the Olsen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:25.520
twins and the Mowry twins, the Sprouse twins are&nbsp;
up there in terms of fame. But while both Dylan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:31.360
and Cole Sprouse worked as child actors, only Cole&nbsp;
has come back to acting full time since they both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:37.200
took a hiatus to attend college. Even then, he&nbsp;
once told Teen Vogue that he almost quit for good.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:41.360
The Sprouses made their acting&nbsp;
debut before he was even a year old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:45.440
when he started appearing as Patrick&nbsp;
Kelly in the sitcom "Grace Under Fire."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.440
He and his brother shared this role, much&nbsp;
like they did with other big gigs like playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:56.000
Ross's son Ben on "Friends" and Adam Sandler's&nbsp;
adopted son Julian in the comedy "Big Daddy."

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.280
"But I did most scenes."

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:58.960
"No I did."

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.120
"No I did! Mommy says I did."

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:02.240
"Did he?"

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:09.600
To kids of their generation, the Sprouse brothers&nbsp;
are perhaps best known as Zack and Cody Martin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:15.040
mischievous twin brothers featured on the Disney&nbsp;
program "The Suite Life of Zack and Cody" and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:21.280
spinoff "The Suite Life on Deck." Cole Sprouse&nbsp;
did not act between 2011 and 2017, mostly to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:26.720
attend New York University and study archeology.&nbsp;
He is now more famous than ever, due to his role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:32.497
as Jughead Jones on The CW's "Riverdale," which&nbsp;
was recently renewed for its seventh season.

00:10:32.601 --> 00:10:37.200
Diane Lane has been a Hollywood heavyweight&nbsp;
for decades. The actor started her screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.889 --> 00:10:49.129
with roles in films such as "Ladies and&nbsp;
Gentleman, the Fabulous Stains," "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.129 --> 00:10:53.929
Outsiders," and "Rumble Fish," before deciding&nbsp;
to step away from acting at the age of 19.

00:10:53.929 --> 00:10:58.489
According to Hello magazine, Lane moved to&nbsp;
Georgia during her hiatus from Hollywood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.489 --> 00:11:01.849
While some sources, like The&nbsp;
Herald, have suggested Lane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.849 --> 00:11:05.849
retired because "The Cotton Club" was&nbsp;
a flop, others have said it had more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.849 --> 00:11:10.169
to do with rebuilding her relationship with&nbsp;
her mother, telling the Los Angeles Times -

00:11:10.169 --> 00:11:14.249
"I hadn't been close to my mom for a long&nbsp;
time, so we had a lot of homework to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.249 --> 00:11:17.369
We had to repair our relationship&nbsp;
because I wanted my mother back."

00:11:17.369 --> 00:11:23.209
Two years after her 1987 return to acting,&nbsp;
she received a Primetime Emmy nomination for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.209 --> 00:11:28.089
her work in the miniseries "Lonesome Dove."&nbsp;
She has since been nominated for many awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.089 --> 00:11:32.569
including an Oscar for her role in the&nbsp;
film "Unfaithful." Other standout projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.569 --> 00:11:37.769
include "A Walk on the Moon," "Trumbo," and&nbsp;
playing Superman's Earth mom, Martha Kent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.769 --> 00:11:41.677
in "Man of Steel" and "Batman&nbsp;
v Superman: Dawn of Justice."

00:11:41.677 --> 00:11:47.529
Gaby Hoffman has lived an unconventional life.&nbsp;
The popular child star grew up in Manhattan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.529 --> 00:11:51.209
Chelsea Hotel, and according to The&nbsp;
Guardian, she started acting at age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.209 --> 00:11:56.809
four to help pay the family's bills. Her&nbsp;
movie debut was in 1989's "Field of Dreams,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.809 --> 00:12:01.529
after which she starred with Hollywood giants&nbsp;
in films like "Sleepless in Seattle" and "Uncle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.529 --> 00:12:06.889
Buck." She was also successful as a teenager, with&nbsp;
performances in "Now and Then," "Everyone Says I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.889 --> 00:12:12.089
Love You," and "200 Cigarettes." Hoffmann&nbsp;
continued appearing in films until 2001,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.089 --> 00:12:15.929
at which point she decided to step&nbsp;
away from the screen, telling NPR -

00:12:15.929 --> 00:12:20.409
"I always was planning on giving it up. I never&nbsp;
set out to be an actor. I always knew, since I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.409 --> 00:12:25.449
was seven or eight years old, that it was a means&nbsp;
to an end and that I wanted to go to college."

00:12:25.449 --> 00:12:27.369
After attending Bard College,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.369 --> 00:12:32.729
Hoffmann was on the fence about acting again, but&nbsp;
eventually decided to try things out as an adult.

00:12:32.729 --> 00:12:34.030
"I saw 'The Maid' at Sundance —"

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:34.283
"Oh yeah."

00:12:34.283 --> 00:12:38.249
"— And I was like, 'Okay, if people&nbsp;
are making movies like this still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.249 --> 00:12:40.889
anywhere in the world, I&nbsp;
wanna keep making movies.'"

00:12:40.889 --> 00:12:45.609
A decade later, she found major success with&nbsp;
a recurring role in the TV series "Girls,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.609 --> 00:12:50.089
receiving one Primetime Emmy nomination,&nbsp;
and a main role on Amazon's "Transparent,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.089 --> 00:12:54.809
receiving two nominations. Hoffmann has also&nbsp;
done some amazing work in smaller films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.809 --> 00:12:58.889
such as "Obvious Child" and other high&nbsp;
profile series, like "Winning Time."

00:12:59.529 --> 00:13:05.129
Alicia Goranson is mostly known for playing the&nbsp;
original Becky Conner on the sitcom "Roseanne,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.129 --> 00:13:10.489
which she started doing in 1988 at age 13.&nbsp;
She remained with the show for five seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.489 --> 00:13:15.849
eventually leaving to attend Vassar College. In&nbsp;
addition to "Roseanne," Goranson appeared in "How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.849 --> 00:13:20.569
to Make an American Quilt," but she was not as&nbsp;
prolific as some other child stars of her era.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.569 --> 00:13:24.409
After leaving "Roseanne" in 1992,&nbsp;
Goranson appeared on the show a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.409 --> 00:13:30.569
more times through 1996, but did not act&nbsp;
again until 1999, telling Parade in 2019 -

00:13:30.569 --> 00:13:34.809
"I really missed just having a regular&nbsp;
life. I craved being in a real classroom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.809 --> 00:13:38.889
and not just a studio schoolroom and having&nbsp;
the opportunity to be around my peers."

00:13:38.889 --> 00:13:42.889
Goranson returned to the business with&nbsp;
a juicy role in the heartbreaking film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.889 --> 00:13:48.809
"Boys Don't Cry," but disappeared again until&nbsp;
she popped up with a guest spot on a 2004 episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.809 --> 00:13:53.609
of "Sex and the City." She continued to work,&nbsp;
but mostly in small guest roles that received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.609 --> 00:13:59.209
little notice. In 2018, Goranson returned&nbsp;
to prominence when "Roseanne" was rebooted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.209 --> 00:14:03.689
After the real-life Roseanne was fired, the&nbsp;
show was renamed "The Conners" in an attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.689 --> 00:14:08.569
to refocus on the rest of the family but&nbsp;
preserve what worked. ABC has renewed "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.569 --> 00:14:14.076
Conners" for Season 5, and Goranson's Becky&nbsp;
remains a big part of the spinoff's focus.

00:14:14.076 --> 00:14:18.569
Thora Birch is now approaching middle age,&nbsp;
but if you remember her as a youngster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.569 --> 00:14:24.009
you are not alone. Birch took on her first film&nbsp;
role at the tender age of six, when she appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.009 --> 00:14:29.529
in "Purple People Eater." She then went on to film&nbsp;
multiple television shows, including "Day by Day"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.529 --> 00:14:34.649
and "Parenthood," but really established herself&nbsp;
as a child star with her film work. Her early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.649 --> 00:14:40.889
films include the Halloween classic "Hocus Pocus,"&nbsp;
coming-of-age drama "Now and Then," and the comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.889 --> 00:14:45.929
"Monkey Trouble." She continued acting throughout&nbsp;
her teenage years and garnered much acclaim with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.929 --> 00:14:52.249
roles in critical faves like "American Beauty" and&nbsp;
"Ghost World." Then the actor somewhat vanished.

00:14:52.249 --> 00:14:56.009
After acting sporadically between 2006 and 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.009 --> 00:15:02.089
Birch appeared to have stopped acting entirely&nbsp;
from 2012 to 2016. This is not surprising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.089 --> 00:15:07.449
given that the actor's father was notoriously&nbsp;
heavy-handed. According to the New York Times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.449 --> 00:15:12.009
Jack Birch was allegedly intrusive on&nbsp;
sets, even after his daughter reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.009 --> 00:15:17.209
adulthood. He was apparently on set when&nbsp;
his daughter filmed a sex scene in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.209 --> 00:15:23.209
at age 25, and even got her fired from a play&nbsp;
in 2010 when he threatened one of her co-stars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.209 --> 00:15:28.329
Given her innate talent, Birch has thankfully&nbsp;
returned to acting in recent years. She dipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.329 --> 00:15:33.609
her toe back in with recurring parts on shows like&nbsp;
"The Walking Dead," and smaller film roles like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.609 --> 00:15:39.343
the disaster movie '13 Minutes' and has a main&nbsp;
role in the upcoming Netflix series, "Wednesday."

00:15:39.343 --> 00:15:44.089
As the daughter of a famous singer, it's not&nbsp;
surprising that actor Mackenzie Phillips, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.089 --> 00:15:49.449
her younger half-siblings Bijou and Chynna, ended&nbsp;
up in the entertainment business. Mackenzie's'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.449 --> 00:15:54.729
early start in the industry led to a life filled&nbsp;
with struggle, including much-publicized addiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.729 --> 00:15:59.449
issues and an incestuous relationship with her&nbsp;
own father which she discussed in her book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.449 --> 00:16:04.249
"High on Arrival". Fortunately, Phillips found&nbsp;
stability and happiness in her later years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.249 --> 00:16:08.649
and she is now working as a counselor for&nbsp;
others struggling with substance addiction.

00:16:08.649 --> 00:16:12.809
"I think that people understand that I&nbsp;
- that I've been there, and that clients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.809 --> 00:16:14.969
believe me when I say there's a way out."

00:16:14.969 --> 00:16:20.009
Phillips began her acting career in&nbsp;
1973 with a small role in a TV film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.009 --> 00:16:24.889
"Doris," and a part in the classic "American&nbsp;
Graffiti." She rose to prominence a couple of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.889 --> 00:16:30.249
years later as rebellious teenager Julie Cooper&nbsp;
on "One Day at a Time," which remains her most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.249 --> 00:16:35.449
significant role to date. Phillips was 25 by&nbsp;
the end of the nine-season run, and she left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.449 --> 00:16:41.929
acting for music a year later in 1986. It was not&nbsp;
until 1994 that she returned to the screen, mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.929 --> 00:16:47.369
for guest spots on shows like "Melrose Place"&nbsp;
and "Caroline in the City." In 1999, Phillips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.369 --> 00:16:52.969
finally found another memorable part, playing a&nbsp;
rocker mom in the Disney show "So Weird." She has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.969 --> 00:16:58.944
also had noteworthy roles on "Orange Is The New&nbsp;
Black" and the "One Day at a Time" reboot series.

